# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Breeze Messenger — Environment Variables
# Copy to Cloudflare Pages > Settings > Environment Variables
# or use: wrangler secret put <KEY>
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ─── Required: KV Binding ────────────────────────────────
# Create via: wrangler kv:namespace create KV
# Then bind in Pages > Settings > Functions > KV namespace bindings
# Name: KV
# KV=<bound automatically>

# ─── Billing (Stripe): REMOVED/NEVER WIRED UP ────────────
# No checkout, webhook, or portal route exists in _worker.js, and nothing calls
# api.stripe.com anywhere in this repo — these variables have no consuming code.
# Multi-account paid tiers are not currently available; see SECURITY.md's
# "Removed/never implemented: multi-account billing" section.

# ─── TURN Server (NAT traversal) ─────────────────────────
# Option A: Cloudflare Calls (recommended, $0.05/GB)
#   Create at: Dashboard > Calls > Create > TURN App
# TURN_KEY_ID=<your-turn-key-id>
# TURN_KEY_API_TOKEN=<your-turn-api-token>
#
# Option B: Custom HMAC (Coturn self-hosted)
# TURN_URL=turn:your-server:3478
# TURN_SECRET=your-hmac-secret
#
# Option C: Static credentials (metered.ca, twilio, etc.)
# TURN_URL=turn:relay.metered.ca:80
# TURN_USERNAME=your-username
# TURN_CREDENTIAL=your-credential
#
# Option D: No config needed → auto-falls to free Open Relay (20GB/month)

# ─── Web Push Notifications ──────────────────────────────
# Generate VAPID keys: npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=BM...
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=<base64-private-key>

# ─── Moderation (optional) ───────────────────────────────
# Notified on verified abuse reports — metadata only (frankId, message length,
# timestamp), never message content.
# ABUSE_WEBHOOK_URL=https://your-moderation-endpoint.example/hook

# ─── Anti-spam tuning (optional) ─────────────────────────
# Server-side proof-of-work acceptance floor for @alias registration.
# Default 20 (matches the client's hardcoded PoW difficulty) if unset.
# MIN_POW_DIFFICULTY=20

# ─── Auth hardening (optional — off by default for zero-config self-hosting) ──
# Every registered client already signs the actions below with its Ed25519 identity
# key; these flags just tell the Worker to REJECT a request that omits/fails that
# signature instead of silently accepting it. Safe to enable once you're not also
# serving pre-upgrade clients that predate this signing (check CHANGELOG.md for when
# each call started signing).
# PRESENCE_REQUIRE_AUTH=true   # reject unsigned presence writes
# ALIAS_REQUIRE_AUTH=false     # opt OUT: allow unsigned @alias registration (signature is REQUIRED by default since recovery-plan step 5; every current client signs)
# GROUP_REQUIRE_AUTH=true      # reject unsigned group create/kick/rename/admin/leave/delete/transfer
# PUSH_REQUIRE_AUTH=true       # reject unsigned push subscribe/unsubscribe
# TURN_REQUIRE_AUTH=true       # reject unsigned TURN credential requests
# QUEUE_REQUIRE_AUTH=true      # reject unsigned msg/sealed poll + sealed ack
#                              # (queue read/delete — knowing a userId is enough to drain it)
# PREKEY_REQUIRE_AUTH=true     # reject unsigned prekey uploads — without it, any
#                              # userId's key bundle can be overwritten (identity takeover)
# BACKUP_REQUIRE_AUTH=true     # without this, knowing a userId is enough to download
#                              # that user's encrypted backup blob
# BACKUP_STORAGE_ENABLED=true  # opt IN to storing users' encrypted backup blobs on
#                              # this relay. Default is OFF (the creator holds no user
#                              # data): /api/backup/* refuses with BACKUP_DISABLED.
#                              # Enable only on a relay you run for your own accounts.

# ─── Relay path kill switch (optional) ───────────────────
# The plain (unsealed) relay path is deprecated — it stores sender id, name,
# public key and group name in plaintext on the server. Set to "true" to make
# /api/msg/send and /api/msg/poll answer 410 PLAIN_PATH_DISABLED outright.
# Only safe once no served client still falls back to the plain path (see
# SECURITY.md for the removal conditions).
# PLAIN_PATH_DISABLED=true

# ─── Registration admission (optional, stage-2 step 3) ──
# REGISTRATION_ALLOWLIST closes ACCOUNT CREATION on this relay to the
# listed Breeze IDs (comma-separated; an ID is the first 12 characters
# of a user's public key — the id in their invite link). Only a prekey
# upload for an ID with no stored bundle is gated: existing accounts
# keep uploading, receiving and polling exactly as before. Unset (the
# default, including the official deployment) registration is open;
# set-but-empty closes it to everyone. The Docker packaging sets this
# (possibly empty) so self-hosts are closed by default — see
# docs/SELF_HOSTING.md. On the Node host, REGISTRATION_OPEN=1 is the
# explicit opt-in that re-opens registration.
# REGISTRATION_ALLOWLIST=id1,id2

# ─── Usage quota / fair use (optional) ─────────────────
# The default relay is a free ENTRANCE with a bounded frame, not an
# unlimited commons: per mailbox, deposits (one deposit = one full
# delivery cycle) are capped per UTC day and month. Past the cap,
# senders get an explicit 429 USAGE_QUOTA_EXCEEDED with the window
# and limit; nothing is silently dropped, and the recipient stays
# reachable over P2P and via their home relay. Policy + numbers:
# docs/COST_MODEL.md §10. Defaults (10,000/day, 200,000/month) sit
# ~50x above a heavy user's measured traffic, so ordinary personal
# use never touches them. Enforced only with MAILBOX_BACKEND=do
# (rides the same per-mailbox deposit gate); a documented no-op on
# the KV backend. If this relay is YOUR OWN frame (self-hosted for
# your own accounts), disable the quota — it exists for the shared
# default entrance, not for you.
# USAGE_QUOTA_ENABLED=false   # disable the quota entirely
# USAGE_QUOTA_DAILY=10000     # deposits per mailbox per UTC day
# USAGE_QUOTA_MONTHLY=200000  # deposits per mailbox per UTC month

# ─── Mailbox storage backend (optional) ──────────────
# Stage-1 step 2: mailboxes (the inbox/sealed queues) can be served from a
# per-recipient Durable Object instead of single KV values — strong
# consistency and per-mailbox serialization instead of KV's
# read-modify-write. Set to "do" to enable; each mailbox drains from KV
# into its Durable Object on first access, then the KV copy is deleted
# (no dual-write window). The default (unset) is "kv", the pre-step-2
# behaviour. The Durable Object only engages when the MAILBOX_DO binding
# (configured in wrangler.toml, not a secret) is also present; a "do"
# value without the binding falls back to KV.
# MAILBOX_BACKEND=do

# ─── Presence storage backend (optional) ─────────────
# Stage-1 step 4: the presence record (one per user, flushed by the
# heartbeat at most once per 5 minutes) can live in the user's own
# MailboxDO instead of KV — with mailboxes moved, presence was the
# largest remaining consumer of the free tier's 1,000 KV writes/day.
# Set to "do" to enable; each user's record drains from KV into their
# Durable Object on first access, then the KV copy is deleted (no
# dual-write window). Independent of MAILBOX_BACKEND: either flag can
# move without the other. The default (unset) is "kv", the pre-step-4
# behaviour; a "do" value without the MAILBOX_DO binding falls back
# to KV. The 5-minute write throttle applies on both backends.
# PRESENCE_BACKEND=do

# ─── WebSocket delivery (optional) ───────────────────
# Stage-1 step 5: a hibernatable WebSocket per recipient wakes the
# client the moment a deposit lands, replacing the 3 s foreground
# poll with a push + slow safety poll. The socket is a wake-up
# channel only — message data always moves via the poll endpoints —
# so clients without the feature (or that cannot produce the
# required Ed25519 ownership signature) keep polling unchanged.
# Effective only together with MAILBOX_BACKEND=do: the wake-up is
# emitted inside the recipient's Durable Object as it stores a
# deposit. The default (unset) serves no WebSocket route at all.
# MAILBOX_WS_ENABLED=true

# ─── Relay identity (stage-2 step 4) ─────────────────────
# A persistent Ed25519 keypair for THIS relay. /api/health advertises
# the public half as `relayKey`; POST /api/relay/attest proves the
# relay holds the private half, and clients pin the verified key
# (substitution detection — see SECURITY.md, "Relay identity"). BOTH
# halves must be set, or no key is advertised and clients keep the
# pre-step-4 implicit trust. The Node host (server/) needs neither:
# it keeps its pair in the database (relay_meta table) on its own.
# Generate a pair in exactly the stored formats:
#   node -e "const c=require('node:crypto');const k=c.generateKeyPairSync('ed25519');console.log('RELAY_PUBLIC_KEY='+Buffer.from(k.publicKey.export({format:'jwk'}).x,'base64url').toString('base64'));console.log('RELAY_PRIVATE_KEY='+k.privateKey.export({format:'der',type:'pkcs8'}).toString('base64'))"
# RELAY_PUBLIC_KEY=     # raw Ed25519 public key, base64
# RELAY_PRIVATE_KEY=    # PKCS#8 DER private key, base64 — treat like a password

# ─── AI / Translation: REMOVED (v3.6.1) ──────────────────
# Server-side AI (Groq/Anthropic/OpenAI) and translation (DeepL/Google/
# LibreTranslate/MyMemory) proxying were deleted outright, not gated: they
# decrypted a message and sent the plaintext off-device, which is
# incompatible with the zero-knowledge relay this project promises. There
# is no env var to re-enable them — see SECURITY.md's "Removed: server-side
# AI and translation" section. /summarize and smart replies still work,
# computed locally on-device with zero network egress.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# $0/month deployment: Set NOTHING above.
# - KV binding is the only requirement (free)
# - TURN auto-falls to free Open Relay
# - Billing disabled (all users get Free plan)
# - Push notifications disabled
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
